---
/* WHAT THE ATLAS IS, IN WORDS, BELOW THE MAP ON THE FRONT PAGE.
 *
 * The front page is the map, and the map is drawn by JavaScript. Read as HTML (which is how a search engine's first pass reads it) the page was
 * a header, a «Loading the atlas…» line and about 370 words, and AdSense reviewed that and called the site «low-value content». This section is
 * ordinary server-rendered text, in the HTML the server sends, below the map: nothing above the fold moves, the map keeps its size and its
 * address parameters, and a visitor who scrolls finds a plain account of what the atlas is and is not.
 *
 * EVERY NUMBER IS COUNTED FROM THE DATA AT BUILD, by the same module that prints the volumes pill (lib/inventory.js), so this text cannot say
 * 57,000 places when the pill says 57,354. Nothing here is typed in as a figure. No ad is placed in or near it (the front page is not in the
 * ad list in Base.astro), and no person is named.
 *
 * test/home-about.test.js reads the built page and fails if the section or its words are missing from the static HTML.
 */
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { places, volumes, countries, archives, archiveCountries } from "../lib/inventory.js";
import regions from "../../public/regions.json";
import { corpus } from "../lib/surname-corpus.js";
import gnFamilies from "../../../data/given-name-families.json";
import placeNames from "../../../data/place-names.json";

const n = (x) => Number(x).toLocaleString("en-GB");
const sur = corpus().surnames;
const surnames = Array.isArray(sur) ? sur.length : Object.keys(sur || {}).length;
const datedNames = Object.keys(placeNames.places || {}).length;
---
<section class="ra-about" id="about-the-atlas" aria-labelledby="ra-about-h">
  <h2 id="ra-about-h">What the Record Atlas is, and how to use it</h2>

  <p>The Record Atlas is a map of where family-history records are kept. Pick a place and it shows which archives and online registers
  cover it, for which years, and what it costs to open them: free, an account, a subscription, or a visit in person. It lists
  <b>{n(places)}</b> places in <b>{n(countries)}</b> countries and territories, <b>{n(volumes)}</b> register volumes, <b>{n(archives.length)}</b>
  archives and <b>{n(regions.regions.length)}</b> regions whose rulers changed over the centuries.</p>

  <p>It is made for anyone who holds a name, a village and a rough date and does not know which door to knock on, especially where a border
  moved and the record may sit in a country the village is no longer in. If you are new to this, <a href={u("/start/")}>Start here</a> is a
  short tour.</p>

  <h3>Four things to try</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Find a place and see who covers it.</b> Search the map above, or <a href={u("/places/")}>browse every place</a>. Each page lists the
    archives and registers that cover it. The <a href={u("/countries/")}>country pages</a> do the same for a whole country.</li>
    <li><b>Check what a place was called in the year of the record.</b> A register that says Fiume before 1945 is describing today's Rijeka.
    <a href={u("/place-names/")}>Place names by year</a> holds {n(datedNames)} places whose names changed, with the dates.</li>
    <li><b>Follow a surname's spellings.</b> <a href={u("/surnames/")}>Surnames</a> covers {n(surnames)} names, the spellings registers have
    used and the countries that record them. <a href={u("/your-name/")}>Given names</a> work the same way: Ivan, Johann and Giovanni are one
    name.</li>
    <li><b>Open your own tree.</b> The <a href={u("/tree-canvas/")}>Tree Canvas</a> draws a GEDCOM file on one canvas and joins each person's
    places to this atlas, and its Compass view plays the family across the map through the years. Your file is read in your browser and is
    never uploaded.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What it does not do</h3>
  <p>It is not a database of people, and it holds no scans of records. It points to the archives and sites that hold them, and it cannot say
  whether your ancestor is in any particular book. Coverage is uneven: some countries are walked volume by volume, others have only their
  national archives. A place with no volumes listed means the atlas has none <i>yet</i>, not that none exist.</p>

  <h3>Where it is going, and where the data comes from</h3>
  <p>Next are more places walked book by book, more spellings and old place names, and fuller pages for countries covered thinly;
  <a href={u("/whats-new/")}>What's new</a> lists each change. The records come from the catalogues of the archives and sites themselves, and
  the gazetteers and name lists from open datasets, each credited on <a href={u("/data/")}>Use the data</a>, where every file the map draws can
  be downloaded. Every number here is counted from those files when the site is built. There are no accounts;
  <a href={u("/privacy/")}>the privacy page</a> says what your browser fetches from elsewhere.</p>
</section>

